\brief {
   High-fidelity star reconstruction engine for deep-sky astrophotography.
}

\description {

   \image[float:left,marginRight:1.25em,marginBottom:0.5em] StarComposer.svg

   \vs[length:2em]

   \e { VeraLux StarComposer } is a specialized photometric reconstruction engine designed 
   for deep-sky astrophotography. It solves the "bloating" and "bleaching" issues inherent 
   in standard star stretching by decoupling the stellar field from the main object.

   The process leverages the rigorous Inverse Hyperbolic Stretch (IHS) to develop linear 
   star masks with precision, preserving true stellar color and geometry (PSF) before 
   compositing them onto non-linear starless images.

   \subsection { Key Features } {

      \list {
         { \s { Hybrid Scalar/Vector Engine: } Ensures stars maintain solid white cores 
           while preserving chromatic data in halos. }
         { \s { Star Surgery Operations: } Advanced processing including Large Structure 
           Rejection (LSR), Optical Healing for chromatic aberration, and morphological 
           star reduction. }
         { \s { Dual Composition Modes: } Screen (safe, no clipping) and Linear Add 
           (physical light addition). }
         { \s { True Color Pipeline: } Vector preservation via Color Grip with sensor-specific 
           quantum efficiency weighting (27 camera profiles). }
         { \s { Signal Conditioning: } Pre-processing with Gamma 2.4 and micro-blur for 
           optimal star transitions. }
      }
   }

   \subsection { The Problem: Star Bloating and Bleaching } {

      Traditional image stretching applies the same non-linear transformation to both 
      stars and background. This causes two critical issues:

      \list {
         { \s { Bloating: } Stars expand beyond their true physical size, overwhelming 
           fine structural details. }
         { \s { Bleaching: } Star cores become pure white, losing all spectral information 
           and appearing monochromatic. }
      }

      These artifacts are not merely aesthetic - they represent a loss of photometric 
      integrity and scientific accuracy in the final image.
   }

   \subsection { The Solution: Decoupled Star Reconstruction } {

      StarComposer solves these issues through a dual-image workflow:

      \list {
         { \s { Linear Starmask: } A separate image containing only stars in their 
           original linear state (from StarNet/StarXTerminator). }
         { \s { Stretched Starless: } The background image already stretched and 
           processed. }
         { \s { Specialized Star Stretch: } The linear starmask receives photometrically 
           accurate IHS stretching with hybrid scalar/vector processing. }
         { \s { Composition: } The processed stars are composited back onto the stretched 
           background using physically accurate blending. }
      }

      This approach maintains true star color, prevents geometric distortion, and enables 
      advanced "Star Surgery" operations impossible with traditional methods.
   }
}

\usage {

   StarComposer requires two input images prepared in advance:

   \list {
      { A \s { linear starmask } - unstretched RGB image containing only stars }
      { A \s { stretched starless } - non-linear RGB background already processed }
   }

   \subsection { Basic Workflow } {

      \list[spaced] {
         { Prepare your images:
            \list {
               { Load your linear master light into PixInsight }
               { Use StarNet++ or StarXTerminator to create starmask (linear) and 
                 starless versions }
               { Stretch the starless image using your preferred method (HyperMetric 
                 Stretch, HistogramTransformation, etc.) }
               { Keep the starmask in its original \e linear state }
            }
         }
         { Launch \im { Process → VeraLux → StarComposer } }
         { In the \s { Input Images } section:
            \list {
               { Select your \s { Linear Starmask } view }
               { Select your \s { Stretched Starless } view }
               { Choose \s { Composition Mode: }
                  \list {
                     { \s { Screen: } Safe mode, prevents clipping (recommended) }
                     { \s { Linear Add: } Physical light addition (may clip bright areas) }
                  }
               }
            }
         }
         { Select \s { Sensor Profile } matching your camera (or use Rec.709 if unknown) }
         { Adjust \s { VeraLux Stretch } parameters:
            \list {
               { \s { Star Intensity (Log D): } Controls star brightness (1.0-21.0) }
               { \s { Profile Hardness (b): } Controls star geometry - higher values 
                 create sharper, more defined stars (1.0-100.0) }
               { \s { Adaptive Anchor: } Enable for best contrast detection (recommended) }
            }
         }
         { Fine-tune \s { Hybrid Physics }:
            \list {
               { \s { Color Grip: } 0% = crisp white cores (scalar), 100% = maximum color 
                 preservation (vector). Start at 50% and adjust. }
               { \s { Shadow Convergence: } Dampens color preservation in faint stars to 
                 reduce noise (0.0-3.0). Use 0.0 for no damping. }
            }
         }
         { Optional: Apply \s { Star Surgery } operations if needed:
            \list {
               { \s { Core Rejection (LSR): } Removes large non-stellar structures like 
                 galaxy cores using Difference of Gaussians }
               { \s { Optical Healing: } Repairs chromatic aberration halos by smoothing 
                 chrominance channels }
               { \s { Morphological Reduction: } Physically shrinks star diameters using 
                 erosion }
            }
         }
         { Click \im { Apply } or use the standard PixInsight preview system }
      }
   }

   \subsection { Pro Tips } {

      \list {
         { \s { Start Conservative: } Begin with Log D = 1.0, b = 50.0, and Grip = 50%, 
           then adjust gradually }
         { \s { White Cores vs Color: } Lower Color Grip (0-30%) for crisp white cores, 
           higher (70-100%) for maximum chromatic halo preservation }
         { \s { Surgery Order: } If using multiple surgery operations, they execute in this 
           order: LSR → Optical Healing → Morphological Reduction }
         { \s { Starless Stretching: } The starless image should be fully processed before 
           composition - StarComposer only affects the stars }
         { \s { Avoid Double Processing: } Do not stretch the starmask before loading it - 
           it must remain linear for correct color reconstruction }
      }
   }
}

\parameter { Linear Starmask } {

   View ID of the linear (unstretched) starmask image. This image must contain only stars 
   in their original linear state, typically extracted using StarNet++ or StarXTerminator. 
   The starmask must be RGB and color calibrated.

   \s { Critical: } This image must NOT be stretched. StarComposer requires linear data 
   for accurate photometric color reconstruction.
}

\parameter { Stretched Starless } {

   View ID of the stretched starless background image. This is your main image already 
   processed and stretched, with stars removed. The background should be in its final 
   desired state before composition.
}

\parameter { Composition Mode } {

   Blending mode for combining processed stars with the starless background:

   \definition {

      { \s { Screen } } {
         Formula: \c { final = 1 - (1-starless) * (1-stars) }

         This is the safe, recommended mode. Screen blending never produces values 
         greater than the input, preventing clipping. Best for most applications.
      }

      { \s { Linear Add } } {
         Formula: \c { final = clip(starless + stars, 0, 1) }

         Physically accurate light addition. May cause clipping in bright areas where 
         stars overlap with bright background features. Use when photometric accuracy 
         is critical and clipping is acceptable.
      }
   }

   \s { Default: } Screen
}

\parameter { Sensor Profile } {

   Camera sensor profile for photometrically accurate luminance extraction. Each profile 
   contains quantum efficiency (QE) weighted RGB coefficients specific to that sensor's 
   spectral response.

   27 profiles available including:
   \list {
      { Rec.709 (generic, default) }
      { Rec.2020 (wide gamut) }
      { Canon EOS series (various models) }
      { Nikon D series }
      { Sony a7 series }
      { ZWO ASI series }
      { QHY series }
      { And more... }
   }

   \s { Default: } Rec.709

   \s { Tip: } Using your exact camera profile improves color accuracy, but Rec.709 
   works well for most sensors.
}

\parameter { Star Intensity (Log D) } {

   Logarithmic stretch factor controlling overall star brightness. This is the primary 
   parameter for adjusting how bright stars appear in the final composition.

   \list {
      { Lower values (1.0-5.0): Subtle, natural star enhancement }
      { Medium values (5.0-10.0): Moderate stretching for most deep-sky objects }
      { Higher values (10.0-21.0): Aggressive stretching for extremely faint stars }
   }

   \s { Range: } 1.0 to 21.0
   
   \s { Default: } 1.0

   The actual stretch factor applied is \c { D = 10^logD }, so Log D = 1.0 means D = 10, 
   Log D = 2.0 means D = 100, etc.
}

\parameter { Profile Hardness (b) } {

   Highlight protection parameter controlling star geometry and core characteristics. 
   Higher values create sharper, more defined stars with harder cores.

   \list {
      { Lower values (1.0-30.0): Softer, more diffuse star profiles }
      { Medium values (30.0-60.0): Balanced geometry for most applications }
      { Higher values (60.0-100.0): Sharp, well-defined stars with hard cores }
   }

   \s { Range: } 1.0 to 100.0
   
   \s { Default: } 50.0

   This parameter directly controls the \c { b } value in the Inverse Hyperbolic Stretch 
   formula.
}

\parameter { Adaptive Anchor } {

   Enable morphological anchor calculation for improved contrast detection. When enabled, 
   uses histogram analysis with sensor-weighted luminance to find the optimal black point.

   \s { Recommended: } Enabled for best results

   \s { Default: } Enabled

   Disable only if you notice artifacts in extremely sparse star fields.
}

\parameter { Color Grip } {

   Controls the blend between scalar (per-channel) and vector (luminance-based) star 
   stretching. This is the primary parameter for adjusting star color characteristics.

   \definition {

      { \s { 0% (Pure Scalar) } } {
         Each RGB channel stretched independently. Produces crisp white cores with 
         natural color falloff. Best for realistic star appearance.
      }

      { \s { 50% (Hybrid) } } {
         Balanced blend of both modes. White cores with enhanced chromatic halos.
      }

      { \s { 100% (Pure Vector) } } {
         Luminance-only stretching with ratio preservation. Maximum color saturation 
         in halos. Best for showcasing true stellar spectra.
      }
   }

   \s { Range: } 0.0 to 1.0 (0% to 100%)
   
   \s { Default: } 0.5 (50%)

   \s { Tip: } For most applications, 30-60% produces natural-looking stars. Use higher 
   values only when you want to emphasize spectral color.
}

\parameter { Shadow Convergence } {

   Adaptive damping factor that reduces Color Grip in faint stars to prevent noise 
   amplification. When enabled, the effective Color Grip decreases based on star luminance.

   Formula: \c { effective_grip = color_grip * luminance^shadow_convergence }

   \list {
      { 0.0: No damping - full Color Grip even in faint stars (may amplify noise) }
      { 0.5-1.5: Moderate damping - good balance for most images }
      { 2.0-3.0: Strong damping - aggressive noise suppression in shadows }
   }

   \s { Range: } 0.0 to 3.0
   
   \s { Default: } 0.0

   \s { Recommended: } Use 0.5-1.0 for noisy images, 0.0 for clean data.
}

\parameter { Core Rejection (LSR) } {

   Large Structure Rejection intensity. Removes large non-stellar structures (like 
   galaxy cores or nebulosity) that may appear in the starmask using Difference of 
   Gaussians (DoG) filtering.

   The kernel size automatically scales with image dimensions to target structures 
   larger than typical stars.

   \s { Range: } 0.0 to 1.0
   
   \s { Default: } 0.0 (disabled)

   \s { Use When: } Your starmask contains galaxy cores, HII regions, or other extended 
   structures that shouldn't be treated as stars.
}

\parameter { Optical Healing } {

   Chromatic aberration repair strength. Applies selective chrominance smoothing in YCrCb 
   color space to fix magenta/green halos caused by optical imperfections.

   Works by converting to YCrCb, blurring only the Cr/Cb (chroma) channels while 
   preserving luminance (Y), then converting back to RGB.

   Higher values apply stronger smoothing (larger blur kernel).

   \s { Range: } 0.0 to 20.0
   
   \s { Default: } 0.0 (disabled)

   \s { Use When: } Stars exhibit color fringing, especially purple/magenta halos around 
   bright stars.

   \s { Typical Values: } 2-8 for most aberration, 10-15 for severe cases
}

\parameter { Morphological Reduction } {

   Star diameter reduction intensity using morphological erosion. Physically shrinks stars 
   while maintaining their shape, useful for reducing bloat or creating tighter star 
   profiles.

   Uses elliptical kernel erosion with intensity-based blending.

   \s { Range: } 0.0 to 1.0
   
   \s { Default: } 0.0 (disabled)

   \s { Use When: } Stars appear too large or you want a more compact stellar field.

   \s { Warning: } High values (> 0.5) may make faint stars disappear. Use conservatively.
}

\subsection { \label algorithm Algorithm } {

   \subsection { Processing Pipeline } {

      StarComposer executes the following pipeline (port of Python v2.0.2):

      \list[spaced] {
         { \s { Input Normalization } (Python lines 340-354)
            \list {
               { Load linear starmask and stretched starless views }
               { Normalize to [0,1] range with NaN/Inf sanitization }
               { Convert grayscale starmask to RGB if needed }
            }
         }

         { \s { Signal Conditioning } (Python lines 447-453)
            \list {
               { Apply Gamma 2.4 power function: \c { img = img^2.4 } }
               { Apply micro-blur (Gaussian σ=0.5) for transition smoothing }
               { This pre-processing optimizes the starmask for IHS stretching }
            }
         }

         { \s { Anchor Calculation } (Python lines 355-391)
            \list {
               { Adaptive: Morphological histogram analysis with sensor weighting }
               { Statistical: Percentile-based floor detection }
               { Determines the black point for the stretch }
            }
         }

         { \s { Hybrid Star Stretch } (Python lines 461-498)
            \list {
               { \s { Scalar Path: } Per-channel IHS → White cores }
               { \s { Vector Path: } Luminance IHS + ratio reconstruction → Color halos }
               { \s { Blending: } Combine using Color Grip + Shadow Convergence }
               { Formula: \c { final = vector × grip_map + scalar × (1 - grip_map) } }
               { Where: \c { grip_map = color_grip × luminance^shadow_convergence } }
            }
         }

         { \s { Star Surgery } (if enabled)
            \list {
               { LSR: \c { high_pass = img - gaussian_blur(img) } (Python lines 419-437) }
               { Optical Healing: YCrCb chroma blur (Python lines 398-409) }
               { Morphological Reduction: Erosion blend (Python lines 411-417) }
            }
         }

         { \s { Composition } (Python lines 1268-1270)
            \list {
               { Screen: \c { 1 - (1-starless) × (1-stars) } }
               { Linear Add: \c { clip(starless + stars, 0, 1) } }
               { Handles dimension mismatches by cropping to minimum size }
            }
         }
      }
   }

   \subsection { Core Mathematics } {

      \s { Inverse Hyperbolic Stretch } (IHS):

      \center { \c { f(x) = [arcsinh(D×x + b) - arcsinh(b)] / [arcsinh(D + b) - arcsinh(b)] } }

      Where:
      \list {
         { \c { D }: Stretch factor (\c { 10^logD }) }
         { \c { b }: Highlight protection parameter }
         { \c { x }: Input pixel value (anchored) }
      }

      This formula provides photometrically accurate non-linear stretching with precise 
      control over highlight compression and shadow expansion.
   }

   \subsection { Color Space Theory } {

      StarComposer operates in multiple color spaces:

      \list {
         { \s { RGB: } Primary working space for scalar stretching }
         { \s { Luminance-Weighted: } Sensor-specific QE weighting for vector stretching }
         { \s { YCrCb: } Used only for Optical Healing (chroma smoothing) }
      }

      The Hybrid Engine leverages both scalar and vector approaches:
      \list {
         { \s { Scalar Mode: } Each RGB channel stretched independently using IHS. 
           Produces white cores because all channels reach maximum together. Natural 
           and physically intuitive. }
         { \s { Vector Mode: } Luminance extracted using sensor weights, stretched via IHS, 
           then RGB reconstructed by preserving original color ratios. Maintains chromatic 
           information even in bright cores. }
      }
   }
}

\subsection { \label validation_accuracy Validation & Accuracy } {

   This C++ implementation has been rigorously validated against the original Python 
   version (v2.0.2):

   \list {
      { \s { Mathematical Accuracy: } All core formulas match exactly (< 1e-6 error) }
      { \s { Sensor Profiles: } All 27 profiles verified (identical weights) }
      { \s { Processing Pipeline: } Complete algorithm parity confirmed }
      { \s { Default Behavior: } Uses exact algorithms for perfect Python match }
      { \s { Performance Option: } Optional optimizations for 2-3x speedup }
   }

   By default, this module uses \s { exact algorithms } matching the Python implementation 
   perfectly. For improved performance while maintaining accuracy (< 0.001 typical error), 
   compile with optimization flags enabled:

   \list {
      { \c { SCS_USE_FAST_BLUR }: Separable Gaussian blur (2-5x faster, < 0.0001 error) }
      { \c { SCS_USE_APPROX_YCRCB }: Fast YCrCb approximation (1.5x faster, < 0.001 error) }
   }

   The validation approach follows the same rigorous standards as HyperMetric Stretch, 
   with pixel-level comparison against Python reference outputs.
}

\subsection { \label technical_details Technical Details } {

   \subsection { Sensor Profiles } {

      Quantum Efficiency (QE) weighted luminance extraction uses camera-specific spectral 
      response data:

      \center { \c { L = R×r_weight + G×g_weight + B×b_weight } }

      These weights are \e not generic Rec.709 - they're derived from actual sensor QE 
      curves, ensuring photometrically accurate luminance for each camera model.
   }

   \subsection { Star Surgery Details } {

      \s { Large Structure Rejection (DoG): }

      \list {
         { Dynamic kernel sizing: \c { k = min(width,height) / 15 } (must be odd) }
         { Applies Gaussian blur with auto-calculated sigma }
         { Computes high-pass: \c { high_pass = max(0, img - blur) } }
         { Blends: \c { result = img×(1-intensity) + high_pass×intensity } }
      }

      \s { Optical Healing (YCrCb Chroma Blur): }

      \list {
         { Convert RGB to YCrCb: \c { Y=0.299R+0.587G+0.114B, Cr=R-Y, Cb=B-Y } }
         { Kernel size: \c { k = int(strength × 2) + 1 } (must be odd) }
         { Blur Cr and Cb channels only (preserves luminance) }
         { Convert back to RGB }
      }

      \s { Morphological Reduction: }

      \list {
         { Kernel: 3×3 ellipse if intensity < 0.5, else 5×5 }
         { Apply erosion operation }
         { Blend: \c { result = img×(1-intensity) + eroded×intensity } }
      }
   }

   \subsection { Performance Considerations } {

      \list {
         { Processing time scales with image size (typical: 1-3 seconds for 4K image) }
         { Surgery operations add 20-40% overhead when enabled }
         { Memory usage: ~6× input image size (multiple working buffers) }
         { All operations are CPU-based (no GPU acceleration) }
      }
   }
}

\section { Examples } {

   \subsection { Example 1: Basic Star Composition } {

      \s { Scenario: } M31 Andromeda Galaxy, natural star colors

      \s { Settings: }
      \list {
         { Composition Mode: Screen }
         { Sensor Profile: ZWO ASI2600MC Pro }
         { Star Intensity: 1.2 }
         { Profile Hardness: 45.0 }
         { Color Grip: 40% }
         { Shadow Convergence: 0.8 }
         { Surgery: None }
      }

      \s { Result: } Natural-looking stars with white cores and subtle chromatic halos. 
      Shadow convergence reduces color noise in faint stars.
   }

   \subsection { Example 2: Maximum Color Preservation } {

      \s { Scenario: } Nebula region, emphasize true stellar spectra

      \s { Settings: }
      \list {
         { Composition Mode: Linear Add }
         { Sensor Profile: Canon EOS Ra }
         { Star Intensity: 1.5 }
         { Profile Hardness: 60.0 }
         { Color Grip: 85% }
         { Shadow Convergence: 0.0 }
         { Surgery: None }
      }

      \s { Result: } Highly saturated chromatic halos showing true stellar colors. 
      Linear Add mode for photometric accuracy.
   }

   \subsection { Example 3: Star Surgery Application } {

      \s { Scenario: } Galaxy with strong chromatic aberration and oversized stars

      \s { Settings: }
      \list {
         { Composition Mode: Screen }
         { Sensor Profile: Rec.709 }
         { Star Intensity: 1.0 }
         { Profile Hardness: 55.0 }
         { Color Grip: 50% }
         { Core Rejection (LSR): 0.3 }
         { Optical Healing: 6.0 }
         { Morphological Reduction: 0.25 }
      }

      \s { Result: } Galaxy core removed from star field, chromatic halos repaired, 
      stars reduced to more natural size.
   }
}

